  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Schools for IDP Resettlement in Katakwi (SIRK) (Uganda)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">For 20 years conflict has shaped the lives of children in Northern Uganda. Many have been confined in refugee camps for their entire childhood and during this time only 10% of children in the region received any form of education. The situation in some areas has improved in recent years and families are starting to leave camps and return to their home areas, but the schools they are returning to are woefully ill prepared. Many children are traumatised by their experience of the conflict and need specialist support and schooling that is relevant to their situation. This project aims to establish a model for education that can be replicated across Northern Uganda. It will improve the quality and relevance of education increasing basic literacy and numeracy rates, but also providing training in skills lost during years of displacement. Parents and communities will be involved in the process with the aim of rebuilding confidence in education and the government as a provider and so increasing attendance rates. District education staff will be supported and trained to ensure sustainability. Over 14,000 pupils will directly benefit, helping to raise the standard of education and reduce the risks of future insecurity. An additional &#xa3;10,000 has been added to the request amount to fund a mid-term and final evaluation which it was felt had been under budgeted for.</narrative>
  </description>
